 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 9,
   "text": "HOLYHEAD MOUNTAIN. 220 m of rock, the highest point on Anglesey, with a Roman lookout on top. In Welsh it's Mynydd Twr. On a clear day you can see Ireland. Today is not the clear day. Hope springs eternal."
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "THE FERRY QUAY. Boats leave here for Dublin. It's about three hours on a good day, and a good deal longer if the Irish Sea has an opinion. Take a flask. Take a good book."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 32,
   "text": "THE BREAKWATER. 1.7 km (more than a mile) of granite, started in 1847 and finished in 1873, taking 26 years. For a time it was the longest in the world. It is also the best place in Wales to stare at the horizon and feel small."
  },
  {
   "x": 48,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "CAER GYBI. A Roman fort of the 4th century, one of the very few with its walls still around a working church. The sea once came right up to it. Cybi was a Cornish saint who founded the church, which means the Celtic world was an extremely good commuter belt."
  }
 ],
